-<div id="print-area"><div id="print-ko" class="print-section"><h1 style="text-align:center;font-size:1.55rem">Knowledge Organiser — Muscles Work in Pairs</h1><p style="text-align:center;margin:2px 0 8px"><strong>BUILD · White Rose Science Year 3 · Week 4</strong></p><table class="ko-table"><tr><th>Key word</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td>muscle</td><td>A soft part of the body that pulls.</td></tr><tr><td>contract</td><td>The muscle gets shorter and tighter. It PULLS.</td></tr><tr><td>relax</td><td>The muscle gets longer and softer. It stops pulling.</td></tr><tr><td>pair</td><td>Two muscles that work together, taking turns.</td></tr><tr><td>joint</td><td>The place where two bones meet and can move.</td></tr></table><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Key facts</h2><ul><li>A muscle can only PULL. It can never push.</li><li>That is why muscles come in pairs — one pulls the bone one way, the other pulls it back.</li><li>Arm bends: biceps contracts, triceps relaxes.</li><li>Arm straightens: triceps contracts, biceps relaxes.</li><li>Bones do not move themselves. The muscle pulls the bone.</li></ul><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Success criteria</h2><ul><li>I can name the two muscles in the arm pair.</li><li>I can say what happens to each muscle when the arm bends.</li><li>I can show the pair working on a model.</li></ul></div><div id="print-intro" class="print-section"><h2>Muscles Work in Pairs</h2><p><strong>Name:</strong> ____________________ &nbsp; <strong>Tier ringed by adult:</strong> ◆ Supported ▲ Standard ★ Stretch &nbsp; <strong>Date:</strong> <span id="print-date"></span></p><div class="rev-block"><h3>Learning objective</h3><p>I can explain that muscles work in pairs to move a bone.</p><h3>Today at a glance</h3><ul><li>Meet the pair</li><li>Make the model move</li><li>Explain the swap</li></ul></div><div style="border:1px solid #999;border-left:6px solid #333;background:#f4f4f5;border-radius:8px;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;font-size:.9rem"><strong>Teacher note —