-<div id="print-area"><div id="print-ko" class="print-section"><h1 style="text-align:center;font-size:1.55rem">Knowledge Organiser — Levers, Pulleys and Gears</h1><p style="text-align:center;margin:2px 0 8px"><strong>GROW · White Rose Science Year 5/6 · Week 4</strong></p><table class="ko-table"><tr><th>Key word</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td>mechanism</td><td>A part that changes a force — its size, its direction, or both.</td></tr><tr><td>lever</td><td>A rigid bar that turns around a pivot.</td></tr><tr><td>pivot</td><td>The fixed point a lever turns around.</td></tr><tr><td>pulley</td><td>A wheel with a rope over it; changes the direction of a pull.</td></tr><tr><td>gear</td><td>A toothed wheel that turns another toothed wheel.</td></tr></table><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Key facts</h2><ul><li>A mechanism does not create energy. It trades distance for force.</li><li>Move the pivot closer to the load and the lever gets easier — but you move the other end further.</li><li>A single fixed pulley changes direction only. Adding pulleys shares the load.</li><li>A small gear driving a big gear turns slower but with more turning force.</li><li>Scissors, wheelbarrows, spanners and door handles are all levers.</li></ul><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Success criteria</h2><ul><li>I can name the three mechanisms and point to one example of each.</li><li>I can identify the pivot on a lever.</li><li>I can explain what a mechanism gives you and what it costs you.</li></ul></div><div id="print-intro" class="print-section"><h2>Levers, Pulleys and Gears</h2><p><strong>Name:</strong> ____________________ &nbsp; <strong>Tier ringed by adult:</strong> ◆ Supported ▲ Standard ★ Stretch &nbsp; <strong>Date:</strong> <span id="print-date"></span></p><div class="rev-block"><h3>Learning objective</h3><p>I can explain how a mechanism lets a smaller force do a bigger job.</p><h3>Today at a glance</h3><ul><li>Find the pivot</li><li>Move it and feel the difference</li><li>Say what it costs</