-<div id="print-area"><div id="print-ko" class="print-section"><h1 style="text-align:center;font-size:1.55rem">Knowledge Organiser — Discover: Cells Under the Microscope</h1><p style="text-align:center;margin:2px 0 8px"><strong>LAUNCH · Edexcel GCSE Biology 1BI0 Foundation · Paper 1 · Topic 1 · Week 3</strong></p><table class="ko-table"><tr><th>Key word</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td>microscope</td><td>An instrument that makes very small things look much bigger.</td></tr><tr><td>magnification</td><td>How many times bigger the image is than the real object.</td></tr><tr><td>resolution</td><td>The smallest gap that can still be seen as two separate things.</td></tr><tr><td>eyepiece lens</td><td>The lens you look through, usually ×10.</td></tr><tr><td>objective lens</td><td>The lens near the slide; you choose ×4, ×10 or ×40.</td></tr></table><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Key facts</h2><ul><li>Cells are far too small to see with the naked eye — we need a microscope.</li><li>Total magnification = eyepiece lens × objective lens. ×10 with ×40 = ×400.</li><li>Magnification makes the image BIGGER; resolution makes it CLEARER. They are not the same.</li><li>A light microscope magnifies to about ×1500; an electron microscope reaches far higher AND has better resolution.</li><li>Blowing an image up past the resolution limit just gives a bigger blur, not more detail.</li></ul><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Success criteria</h2><ul><li>I can name the two lenses that do the magnifying.</li><li>I can state that magnification makes the image bigger and resolution makes it clearer.</li><li>I can explain why a bigger magnification is not always a better image.</li></ul></div><div id="print-intro" class="print-section"><h2>Discover: Cells Under the Microscope</h2><p><strong>Name:</strong> ____________________ &nbsp; <strong>Tier ringed by adult:</strong> ◆ Supported ▲ Standard ★ Stretch &nbsp; <strong>Date:</strong> <span id="print-date"></span></p><div class="rev-block"><h3>Learning objective</h3><p>I can describe how a light microscope magnifies cells, and why resolution matters as well as magnification.</p><h3>Today at a glance</h3><ul><li>Name the lenses</li><li>Magnify vs resolve</li><li>Why bigger isn't always better</li></ul></div></div><div id="print-glance" class="print-section"><h2>Today at a Glance</h2><ol><li>Name the lenses</li><li>Magnify vs resolve</li><li>Why bigger isn't always better</li></ol><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div><div id="print-arrival" class="print-section"><h2>Arrival task</h2><div class="supported-content"><p>From the word bank, label the lens you look through and the lens near the slide.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div><div class="standard-content"><p>Name the two lenses on a microscope and say which one you look through.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div><div class="stretch-content"><p>Name the two lenses, and work out the total magnification of a ×10 eyepiece with a ×40 objective.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-wedo" class="print-section prevent-break"><h2>We Do — Sort each card into the right group</h2><p>Magnifies to about ×1500 &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Reaches far higher magnification &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Better resolution, finer detail &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Uses two glass lenses and light &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Found in most school labs &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Makes the image bigger but no clearer &rarr; ______________________________</p><h2 style="margin-top:14px">WAGOLL</h2><p>A light microscope uses two lenses and light to magnify cells to about ×1500. An electron microscope gives both higher magnification and better resolution, so it shows much finer detail.</p></div><div id="print-scaffold-supported" class="print-section"><h2>Scaffolding — ◆ Supported</h2><div style="border:1px solid #999;border-left:6px solid #7A5C9E;background:#7A5C9E14;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;margin:8px 0 12px;font-size:.9rem"><strong>Adult notes — access &amp; sensory</strong><br>Microscope work means close focus, glassware and queueing for equipment. Agree who handles glass before the lesson. A pupil who records, calculates or photographs the result is doing assessed work — handling the slide is not the science.</div><div class="scaffold-print-box"><h3>Sentence stems</h3><p>You run the loop, not me. Focus the slide, then DRAW what you actually see in a circle — not what the textbook shows — and label it with the total magnification you used. The drawing plus the magnification is the evidence. Say the sentence as you write it: 'I used a total magnification of ____ and I could see ____.'</p><h3>Word bank</h3><p>microscope, magnification, resolution, eyepiece lens, objective lens</p></div></div><div id="print-scaffold-standard" class="print-section"><h2>Scaffolding — ▲ Standard</h2><div class="scaffold-print-box"><h3>Sentence stems</h3><p>You run the loop, not me. Focus the slide, then DRAW what you actually see in a circle — not what the textbook shows — and label it with the total magnification you used. The drawing plus the magnification is the evidence. Say the sentence as you write it: 'I used a total magnification of ____ and I could see ____.'</p><h3>Word bank</h3><p>microscope, magnification, resolution, eyepiece lens, objective lens</p></div></div><div id="print-scaffold-stretch" class="print-section"><h2>Scaffolding — ★ Stretch</h2><div class="scaffold-print-box"><h3>Sentence stems</h3><p>You run the loop, not me. Focus the slide, then DRAW what you actually see in a circle — not what the textbook shows — and label it with the total magnification you used. The drawing plus the magnification is the evidence. Say the sentence as you write it: 'I used a total magnification of ____ and I could see ____.'</p><h3>Word bank</h3><p>microscope, magnification, resolution, eyepiece lens, objective lens</p></div></div><div id="print-worksheet-supported" class="print-section"><h2>Independent Work — ◆ Supported</h2><div class="prevent-break"><p><strong>Task:</strong> Label a microscope diagram from the word bank, then write which lens you look through and what magnification does.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-worksheet-standard" class="print-section"><h2>Independent Work — ▲ Standard</h2><div class="prevent-break"><p><strong>Task:</strong> Label the microscope, calculate three total magnifications, and write one sentence on the difference between magnification and resolution.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-worksheet-stretch" class="print-section"><h2>Independent Work — ★ Stretch</h2><div class="prevent-break"><p><strong>Task:</strong> Label the microscope, calculate the magnifications, and explain why an electron microscope shows detail a light microscope cannot, using the word resolution.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-exit" class="print-section"><h2>Exit Ticket</h2><p>What is the difference between magnification and resolution?</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div><div id="print-witness" class="print-section"><h1 style="text-align:center;font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:2px">Assessor Witness Statement</h1><p style="text-align:center;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:.88rem"><strong>Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Biology 1BI0 (Foundation, grades 1–5); GCSE Combined Science 1SC0 F where appropriate; AQA UAS science units</strong><br>AQA UAS unit code: TBC (Cheryl). Records what was observed, and at which level of independence.</p><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.93rem;margin-bottom:10px"><tr><td style="padding:7px 8px;border:1px solid #999;width:34%"><strong>Pupil</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 8px;border:1px solid #999">____________________</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 8px;border:1px solid #999;width:34%"><strong>Lesson</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 8px;border:1px solid #999">Discover: Cells Under the Microscope — LAUNCH Science · Week 3</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 8px;border:1px solid #999;width:34%"><strong>Date</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 8px;border:1px solid #999">____________________</td></tr></table><p style="margin:12px 0 4px;font-weight:800;font-size:.95rem">Observed in this lesson</p><p style="margin:0 0 5px">☐ Named the eyepiece and objective lenses.</p><p style="margin:0 0 5px">☐ Distinguished magnification from resolution.</p><p style="margin:0 0 5px">☐ Produced a labelled drawing with the magnification recorded.</p><p style="margin:12px 0 4px;font-weight:800;font-size:.95rem">Assessor comment</p><div style="border:1px solid #999;height:26px;margin-bottom:5px"></div><div style="border:1px solid #999;height:26px;margin-bottom:5px"></div><p style="margin:12px 0 4px;font-weight:800;font-size:.95rem">Ring the tier the pupil actually worked at</p><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.88rem"><tr><td style="padding:9px;border:2px solid #333;width:33.3%;text-align:center"><strong>◆ Supported</strong></td><td style="padding:9px;border:2px solid #333;width:33.3%;text-align:center"><strong>▲ Standard</strong></td><td style="padding:9px;border:2px solid #333;width:33.3%;text-align:center"><strong>★ Stretch</strong></td></tr></table><p style="margin:8px 0 0;font-size:.82rem">Assessor name ______________ Role ____________ Signature ______________ Date ________</p></div><div id="print-lundy" class="print-section"><h2>Lundy Loop</h2><p><strong>Space:</strong> Close focus and queueing for the microscope can be a lot. You can be the one who records and draws while a partner focuses — the drawing is the assessed part.</p><p><strong>Voice:</strong> If your image will not come clear, say so — blurry might mean you have run past the resolution, and that is worth knowing.</p><p><strong>Audience:</strong> Your labelled drawing goes into your Topic 1 folder and is revisited at W7.</p><p><strong>Influence:</strong> The class decides which specimen slide we all look at next lesson.</p><p style="font-size:.95rem;margin:.5rem 0 0"><strong>Closing the loop:</strong> this one ends on paper &mdash; your own hand, or your words scribed by an adult. Saying it aloud starts the loop; writing it is what closes it. Pass is always allowed.</p><p style="font-size:.9rem;color:#444;margin:.4rem 0 .2rem">What I said, and what it changed:</p><div class="print-line"></div><div class="print-line"></div></div><div id="print-feedback" class="print-section"><h2 style="text-align:center">Discover: Cells Under the Microscope — Feedback Sheet</h2><p style="font-size:.95rem;color:#444">Keep with your portfolio.</p><table class="ko-table"><tr><td style="width:50%"><strong>Pupil name:</strong></td><td><strong>Date:</strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan="2"><strong>WWW (What Went Well):</strong><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></td></tr><tr><td colspan="2"><strong>EBI (Even Better If):</strong><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Level worked at:</strong> Supported / Standard / Stretch</td><td><strong>Evidence filed:</strong> Yes / Not yet</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2"><strong>Pupil response — my next step:</strong><div class='print-line'></div></td></tr></table></div></div><script>
