-<div id="print-area"><div id="print-ko" class="print-section"><h1 style="text-align:center;font-size:1.55rem">Knowledge Organiser — Use: Active Transport in Root Hairs and the Gut</h1><p style="text-align:center;margin:2px 0 8px"><strong>LAUNCH · Edexcel GCSE Biology 1BI0 Foundation · Paper 1 · Topic 1 · Week 6</strong></p><table class="ko-table"><tr><th>Key word</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td>root hair cell</td><td>A plant cell with a long thin hair that absorbs water and minerals.</td></tr><tr><td>mineral ion</td><td>A dissolved nutrient a plant needs, e.g. nitrate, from the soil.</td></tr><tr><td>villi</td><td>Tiny folds in the small intestine that absorb digested food.</td></tr><tr><td>uptake</td><td>The rate at which a cell takes a substance in.</td></tr><tr><td>oxygen</td><td>Needed for respiration, which powers active transport.</td></tr></table><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Key facts</h2><ul><li>Root hair cells absorb mineral ions from dilute soil, where the ions are LESS concentrated than inside — so it must be active transport.</li><li>The gut absorbs glucose into the blood even when blood glucose is already higher — against the gradient, so active transport.</li><li>Both root hair and gut cells contain MANY mitochondria to supply the energy.</li><li>If oxygen is removed, respiration slows, less energy is made, and the rate of uptake falls.</li><li>A graph of uptake against oxygen shows uptake rising as oxygen rises — the link to respiration.</li></ul><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Success criteria</h2><ul><li>I can explain how a root hair cell absorbs mineral ions against the gradient.</li><li>I can explain how the gut absorbs glucose when the blood already has more.</li><li>I can read a graph to show active transport depends on respiration.</li></ul></div><div id="print-intro" class="print-section"><h2>Use: Active Transport in Root Hairs and the Gut</h2><p><strong>Name:</strong> ____________________ &nbsp; <strong>Tier ringed by adult:</strong> ◆ Supported ▲ Standard ★ Stretch &nbsp; <strong>Date:</strong> <span id="print-date"></span></p><div class="rev-block"><h3>Learning objective</h3><p>I can apply active transport to root hair and gut cells, and use data to link it to respiration.</p><h3>Today at a glance</h3><ul><li>Root hair uptake</li><li>Gut glucose uptake</li><li>Read the oxygen graph</li></ul></div></div><div id="print-glance" class="print-section"><h2>Today at a Glance</h2><ol><li>Root hair uptake</li><li>Gut glucose uptake</li><li>Read the oxygen graph</li></ol><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div><div id="print-arrival" class="print-section"><h2>Arrival task</h2><div class="supported-content"><p>From the word bank, name the part of the plant cell that absorbs mineral ions.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div><div class="standard-content"><p>Why can a root hair cell not use diffusion to absorb mineral ions?</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div><div class="stretch-content"><p>Explain why a root hair cell must use active transport rather than diffusion for minerals.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-wedo" class="print-section prevent-break"><h2>We Do — Match each card</h2><p>Long thin root hair &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Many mitochondria &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Carrier proteins in the membrane &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Villi in the small intestine &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Uptake rises with oxygen &rarr; ______________________________</p><p>Minerals sucked in like a straw &rarr; ______________________________</p><h2 style="margin-top:14px">WAGOLL</h2><p>A root hair cell absorbs mineral ions by active transport because the ions are more concentrated inside the cell than in the soil. Moving them against the gradient needs energy from respiration, so the cell has many mitochondria. When oxygen is low, respiration and uptake both fall.</p></div><div id="print-scaffold-supported" class="print-section"><h2>Scaffolding — ◆ Supported</h2><div style="border:1px solid #999;border-left:6px solid #7A5C9E;background:#7A5C9E14;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;margin:8px 0 12px;font-size:.9rem"><strong>Adult notes — access &amp; sensory</strong><br>This topic is model- and data-based — comparison tables and graphs. A pupil who reads the data, completes the table or explains the pattern is doing the assessed science.</div><div class="scaffold-print-box"><h3>Sentence stems</h3><p>You explain the gut, out loud as you write: 'Glucose is absorbed into the blood by active transport because its concentration is already higher in the blood than in the gut. Moving it against the gradient needs energy from respiration, so gut cells have many mitochondria.' Direction, reason, source. Then read the oxygen graph the same way.</p><h3>Word bank</h3><p>root hair cell, mineral ion, villi, uptake, oxygen</p></div></div><div id="print-scaffold-standard" class="print-section"><h2>Scaffolding — ▲ Standard</h2><div class="scaffold-print-box"><h3>Sentence stems</h3><p>You explain the gut, out loud as you write: 'Glucose is absorbed into the blood by active transport because its concentration is already higher in the blood than in the gut. Moving it against the gradient needs energy from respiration, so gut cells have many mitochondria.' Direction, reason, source. Then read the oxygen graph the same way.</p><h3>Word bank</h3><p>root hair cell, mineral ion, villi, uptake, oxygen</p></div></div><div id="print-scaffold-stretch" class="print-section"><h2>Scaffolding — ★ Stretch</h2><div class="scaffold-print-box"><h3>Sentence stems</h3><p>You explain the gut, out loud as you write: 'Glucose is absorbed into the blood by active transport because its concentration is already higher in the blood than in the gut. Moving it against the gradient needs energy from respiration, so gut cells have many mitochondria.' Direction, reason, source. Then read the oxygen graph the same way.</p><h3>Word bank</h3><p>root hair cell, mineral ion, villi, uptake, oxygen</p></div></div><div id="print-worksheet-supported" class="print-section"><h2>Independent Work — ◆ Supported</h2><div class="prevent-break"><p><strong>Task:</strong> Label the root hair cell, then complete two sentences on why it uses active transport, from the word bank.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-worksheet-standard" class="print-section"><h2>Independent Work — ▲ Standard</h2><div class="prevent-break"><p><strong>Task:</strong> Explain root hair and gut uptake, then describe what the oxygen–uptake graph shows.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-worksheet-stretch" class="print-section"><h2>Independent Work — ★ Stretch</h2><div class="prevent-break"><p><strong>Task:</strong> Explain both examples, interpret the oxygen–uptake graph, and predict what happens to uptake if the roots are waterlogged.</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div></div><div id="print-exit" class="print-section"><h2>Exit Ticket</h2><p>Why does the gut use active transport, not diffusion, to absorb glucose?</p><div class='print-line'></div><div class='print-line'></div></div><div id="print-witness" class="print-section"><h1 style="text-align:center;font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:2px">Assessor Witness Statement</h1><p style="text-align:center;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:.88rem"><strong>Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Biology 1BI0 (Foundation